          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How is blood pressure actually measured, and why is more than one reading needed?",
          "answer": "Blood pressure is measured as two numbers: the systolic pressure, which is when the heart contracts, and the diastolic pressure, which is the pressure between heartbeats. Both are measured in millimeters of mercury (mm Hg). This information comes from \"Hypertension (high blood pressure)\".\n\nMultiple blood pressure readings are necessary because blood pressure naturally changes throughout the day. Factors like activity, stress, pain, caffeine intake, sleep, and time of day can all influence your blood pressure. It is typically lowest during sleep and rises before you wake up. Due to this variation, a diagnosis of high blood pressure (hypertension) is not made from a single reading. Instead, it requires an average of two or more readings taken on two or more separate occasions.\n\nMeasuring blood pressure outside of a clinic, such as at home or with a 24-hour monitor, can help confirm a diagnosis and identify specific patterns like \"white-coat hypertension\" (when readings are high only in a clinical setting) or \"masked hypertension\" (when in-clinic readings are normal but out-of-clinic readings are high). A single high reading, without a diagnosis, is considered a finding rather than a disease.\n\nThis information is based on \"Hypertension (high blood pressure)\", \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets\", and \"Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family\".\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
